<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · 1861 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">The Bonnie Blue Flag</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A marching song of 1861 for the Confederate States, the Southern states that broke away from the United States in the Civil War.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Harry McCarthy</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>not known (the melody of &quot;The Irish Jaunting Car&quot;)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Confederate States of America</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Jackson, Mississippi, and New Orleans, Louisiana, United States</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1861 (first sung in Jackson, Mississippi, spring 1861; published in New Orleans, 1861)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics by Harry McCarthy, published 1861; melody of &quot;The Irish Jaunting Car&quot;)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonnie-blue-flag">Bonnie Blue Flag</a>&quot;, also known as &quot;We Are a Band of Brothers&quot;, is a marching song of 1861 associated with the Confederate States of America. The entertainer Harry McCarthy wrote the words and set them to the melody of &quot;The Irish Jaunting Car&quot;. The title refers to the Bonnie Blue Flag, the unofficial first flag of the Confederacy. McCarthy first sang it at a concert in Jackson, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, in the spring of 1861, and again that September at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> Academy of Music for the First <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> Volunteer Infantry regiment. The New Orleans publisher A. E. Blackmar issued six editions between 1861 and 1864, with three further arrangements.</p><p>The song names the seceding states one by one and so serves as a list of them, though for the sake of the metre it changes the order in which they left the Union, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> on 20 December 1860 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a> on 8 June 1861. Its editions differ: the 1861 Blackmar edition held by the Library of Congress gives the Confederates' cause as liberty, while another 1861 edition, held by Duke University's library, gives it as property gained by honest toil; eleven other editions were published with different words. When Union Major General Benjamin Butler captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> he is said to have arrested Blackmar, fined him $500, destroyed the copies of the music and fined anyone caught singing or whistling the song $25. Annie Chambers Ketchum, a Confederate widow, published new verses as &quot;The Gathering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>&quot;.</p><p>The Union answered with its own versions. J. L. Geddes, a British-born colonel, wrote &quot;The Bonnie Flag With the Stripes and Stars&quot; in 1863, singing of the Union and of equality, and a Mrs C. Sterett published another in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>; the 69th New York regiment of the Irish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a> sang its &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of the Irish Volunteers&quot; to the same tune. After the war the song lived on in recordings, such as Polk Miller and his Old South Quartet's cylinder of 1913, and in films of the Civil War and the West, from Gone with the Wind (1939) and The Searchers (1956) to Gettysburg (1993) and Gods and Generals (2003).</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A rallying song of the Confederacy that presents the seceding states as a band of brothers joining one by one under a single flag to fight for their cause. Its mood is proud and martial.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>We are a band of brothers, and native to the soil,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Fighting for our liberty with treasure, blood, and toil;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And when our rights were threatened, and the cry rose near and far,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hurrah! for the Bonnie Blue Flag, that bears a single star!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hurrah! Hurrah!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For Southern rights, hurrah!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hurrah! for the Bonnie Blue Flag that bears a single star!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>As long as the Union was faithful to her trust</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Like friends and like brothers, kind we were and just</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But now, when Northern treachery attempts our rights to mar</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>We hoist on high the Bonnie Blue Flag that bears a single star.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>First gallant South Carolina nobly made the stand,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Then came Alabama, who took her by the hand;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Next quickly, Mississippi, Georgia and Florida,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>All raised on high the Bonnie Blue Flag that bears a single star.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Ye men of valor gather round the banner of the Right</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Texas and fair Louisiana join us in the fight</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>and Davis serve, our loved President, and Stephens, statesman rare</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Now rally round the Bonnie Blue Flag that bears a single star.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Now here's to brave Virginia, the Old Dominion State,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With the young Confederacy at length has linked her fate,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Impelled by her example, now other states prepare</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To hoist on high the Bonnie Blue Flag that bears a single star.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Then cheer, boys, cheer, raise a joyous shout</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For Arkansas and North Carolina now have both gone out,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And let another rousing cheer for Tennessee be given,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The single star of the Bonnie Blue Flag has grown to be eleven.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Then here's to our Confederacy, strong we are and brave,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Like patriots of old we'll fight, our heritage to save;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And rather than submit to shame, to die we would prefer,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>So cheer for the Bonnie Blue Flag that bears a single star.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Harry McCarthy (1861). Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Bonnie_Blue_Flag" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Gathering Song (Annie Chambers Ketchum)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new verses</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Bonnie Flag With the Stripes and Stars (J. L. Geddes)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1863</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Union answer song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Union version by Mrs C. Sterett</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Union answer song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Song of the Irish Volunteers (69th New York regiment)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Polk Miller and his Old South Quartet</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1913</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Irish Jaunting Car</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">St Crispin's Day speech, Henry V (William Shakespeare)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the &quot;band of brothers&quot; phrase recalls it</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec"><h2>People, places and events</h2><div class="tih-tags"><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Harry McCarthy</span><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">A. E. Blackmar</span><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Benjamin Butler</span><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Annie Chambers Ketchum</span><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">J. L. Geddes</span><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Polk Miller</span><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Jackson, Mississippi</span><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">secession of the Southern states (1860–1861)</span><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">capture of New Orleans</span></div></section>
